2100:#### 7.5.5 The drain: bounded, consuming, with a persisted cursor
2101-
2102-One public `(admission/drain admission budget)` returns the successor
2103-admission state. It reads the response medium with `stream/next` from
2104-`:pair :cursor`, at most `budget` elements, and persists the cursor past
2105-everything it read. Every read consumes; nothing peeks.
2106-
2107-- **Admission id comparison is keyed by `[link-id serial]`.** `:asked` is
2108-  keyed by link id, and an answer by its full admission id. An answer `a`
2109-  is kept only when its id equals the retained question's id for the
2110-  answer's link id:
2111-
2112-  ```clojure
2113-  (= (:yin.admit/id a)
2114-     (:yin.admit/id (get asked (first (:yin.admit/id a)))))
2115-  ```
2116-
2117-  and `:answers` holds nothing under that id yet. An answer with a stale
2118-  serial for a live link id, a duplicate (the first answer wins), and an
2119-  answer for an unknown or retired id are consumed and dropped.
2120-- On `:dao.stream/gap` the drain adopts the recovery cursor the transport
2121-  returns (the earliest retained position, never an evicted one) and
2122-  continues within the budget. On `:dao.stream/blocked` or
2123-  `:dao.stream/end` it stops.
2124:- Each `round` (7.5.6) drains exactly once, with `serve-budget` (64), at
2125-  its start. After AL-2 nothing else drains; until then the AD-1 hook
2126-  `recheck-on-answers` drains with the same function and budget.
2127-- The media have capacity `pair-capacity` (1024) and evict the oldest
2128-  element. An answer is lost when the unread backlog on the response
2129-  medium exceeds that capacity; host composition must bound the
2130-  accumulated backlog through its production rate and its drain cadence.
2131-  A lost answer leaves its question pending until retirement. There is no
2132-  re-answer protocol.
2133-
2134:#### 7.5.6 One round
2135-
2136-`(interpreter/round linker node budget)` (AL-2) is the linker's unit of
2137-work over one `:linker` value, in this order:
2138-
2139:1. Drain answers: one bounded drain (7.5.5).
2140:2. Serve link requests, stopping at the first pending one (7.5.3).
2141-3. Serve the in-process admission interpreter, when the value holds one
2142:   (7.5.8).
2143-
2144-It returns the successor value, the node, the pending entries and a
2145-progress flag. **Progress** is any of: a link response appended, a
2146-question appended, an in-process answer appended, or a drained answer
2147-that matched a retained question. A drained answer that matched nothing
2148-is consumed and is not progress. The successor is always carried, with
2149-or without progress: the drain's cursor may advance while consuming
2150-irrelevant answers, and the serial is preserved unless a question is
2151-successfully appended.
2152-
2153-Until AL-2, `drive-links` runs `admission/serve` right after the link
2154-`serve` and assocs the admission successor before the VM runs; the link
2155-serve drains at the start of each of its rounds.
2156-
2157:#### 7.5.7 The tick-to-VM handoff
2158-
2159-A round that delivers a link response advances the request cursor past
2160-the parked request. If the VM is not then run, the next round finds no
2161-work and reports no progress, and a driver that runs the VM only on its
2162-own round's progress strands the delivered response. The handoff closes
2163-that gap: **the tick retains the round's successor and passes its
2164-progress into the pending driver, which runs the VM once before
2165-requiring another round to report progress.**
2166-
2167-One host hook, `(recheck-on-tick state events)`, replaces both
2168-`recheck-on-load-events` and `recheck-on-answers` (AL-2). Per tick:
2169-
2170-1. Run one `round` on the session's `:linker` with the session node, and
2171-   assoc the successor and the node into the state before anything else,
2172-   so a raising VM run carries them.
2173-2. If the round reported progress, call the pending driver with that
2174-   progress carried in. `drive-links` gains an initial-progress argument:
2175-   its first iteration runs the VM once with no round of its own (no
2176-   second drain before that run), then continues the existing loop,
2177-   bounded by `link-round-budget`.
2178-3. Else, if the events hold a `:loaded` or `:load-failed` event for a
2179-   manifest a pending link waits on, or an installed head of an awaited
2180-   principal, re-check as today (8.2). The no-progress `:checks` counter
2181-   and the link policy are unchanged.
2182-4. Else nothing happens: no re-check and no `:checks` change.
2183-
2184-Branches 2 and 3 are exclusive, so one tick never re-checks twice.
2185-`yin.repl.main/step-all` makes one `recheck-on-tick` call per tick and
2186-collects one returned text.
2187-
2188-**Error rollback.** The link raise carries one `::linker` key, the whole
2189-interpreter value, and `carry-served` restores it (AL-2). After an
2190-install that raises, the asked entry is dropped, the answer consumed, and
2191-the request, answer and question cursors, the serial and the settled
2192-state stay as the round left them. `(abandon)` uses `retire` on the live
2193-value instead. Until AL-2 the raise carries the link pair and the
2194-admission successor as two keys.
2195-
2196:#### 7.5.8 Attachment and the interpreter contract
2197-
2198-The admission interpreter answers each question with
2199-`(verify decision context question)`: nil allows, a reason map refuses.
2200-It is composed from `{:trusted #{...} :verify f :decision d
2201-:decision-medium m}`. It attaches in one of three ways, none of which
2202-edits the linker:
2203-
2204-- **In process, the default.** The `:admission` configuration builds the
2205-  policy: `trusted-asserter` over the own principal plus `:trusted`, or a
2206-  composed `:verify` over a static `:decision` or a capacity-1
2207:  `:decision-medium`. `round` serves it (7.5.6, step 3).
2208-- **External.** `:admission :external` mints the admission pair and runs
2209-  no interpreter in process. Another process reads the questions and
2210-  appends the answers over any `dao.stream` exposure of the pair. There
2211-  is no privileged node: the external interpreter is a peer of the
2212-  medium like any other.
2213-- **ShiBi.** A ShiBi interpreter attaches as either: a verify function
2214-  over its own published snapshot, in process, or an external reader of
2215-  the questions.
2216-
2217-**The context is the holder.** "Who is asking" is a composition-owned
2218-holder context the interpreter receives as the `context` argument of
2219-`verify`; it never travels in a datum. Today `admission/context` builds
2220-it from the session: `{:yin.admit/subject "ed25519:<hex>" | nil,
2221-:yin.admit/session <token>}`, the subject being the own key's principal.
2222-`:yin.admit/session` stays through AL-1 and AL-2. AL-3 replaces it: the
2223-composition gains an optional `:holder` (a principal id, or a token the
2224-verify understands), `context` answers the subject from it, defaulting to
2225-the own key's principal, and `:yin.admit/session` is deleted.
2226-Authorization vocabulary stays in the context and the policy; the
2227-question and the linker carry none.
2228-
2229-- **Composition-bounded synchronous verification.** `verify` runs
2230-  synchronously inside one round on one snapshot. Its cost per question
2231-  is bounded by the composition, and the interpreter answers at most
2232-  `serve-budget` questions per round. `verify` opens, fetches and waits
2233-  for nothing; a rule that would need a remote read is a rule the
2234-  decision snapshot must already hold (`dao.shibi.md`, limits of the
2235-  adapter contract).
2236-- **The snapshot.** The decision snapshot is the static `:decision`
2237-  value, or the newest value on a capacity-1 `:decision-medium`, read once
2238-  per round. An **absent** snapshot gives no answer: the question stays
2239-  pending and the round reports no progress. An **ended** snapshot
2240-  refuses `:yin.admit/decision-ended`, with no load started.
2241-- **The default interpreter.** The decision is the holder's own
2242-  principal together with the `:trusted` set. It allows when some
2243-  asserter of the question is in that set, and otherwise refuses
2244-  `:yin.admit/untrusted-principal` naming the asserters.
2245-- **Composition.** Policies compose as verify functions; the first reason
2246-  wins. One interpreter writes the response medium.
2247-- **Reconfiguration** (AL-3). `(interpreter/reconfigure linker config)`
2248-  replaces the policy of a `:linker` value and preserves the
2249-  interpreter's cursors, retained questions, serials and external mode.
2250-- **At the REPL, configuration only.** `create-state` takes `:admission`
2251-  beside `:link-policy`, defaulting to the own key from `:dht-key`;
2252-  `--dht-trust` sets its `:trusted`; `:external` selects the external
2253-  attachment. AL-4 adds `(trust p)`, `(untrust p)` and `(trusted)`: they
2254-  edit the `:trusted` configuration and call `reconfigure`, and
2255-  `(trusted)` prints the own principal and the trusted set. `(reset)`
2256-  drops the trusted set unless it was given in `create-state`.
2257-
2258-This is not the gate seam of `dao.shibi.md`. It reuses the `verify`
2259-signature and the snapshot, but a request read off a stream and answered
2260-with one decision per request is the path `dao.shibi.md` names as a
2261-non-goal for the gate, to be added beside `verify`. This section is that
2262-addition, and the linker is its first consumer.
2263-
2264:#### 7.5.9 Authority versus admission
2265-
2266-Two independent inputs. The declared principals (R8, 7.1) decide which
2267-assertions count in the fold, and so what a name resolves to. The
2268-trusted principals decide which resolved entries the holder accepts. A
2269-principal can be declared and untrusted (its names resolve and are
2270-asked, and the default interpreter refuses them) or trusted and
2271-undeclared (its assertions never enter the fold, so nothing of its is
2272-asked). A `:composition` provenance entry is never asked.
2273-
2274:#### 7.5.10 Language-blindness
2275-
2276-All languages compile to the universal AST; neither `yin.vm` nor the
2277-linker knows the source language. No linker datum carries a language, a
2278-frontend or an `[id revision]`: there is no importer key in the question,
2279-the answer or the link request, the closed request key set is unchanged,
2280-and no kernel constructor or `spawn-module` changes. Admission is about
2281-principals (the signing keys that asserted a name), addresses (A, the
2282-only code identity) and names. A published module is AST at an address;
2283-any frontend may import it when the holder's policy admits that
2284-principal for that address. Registry reuse between frontends is not a
2285-linker security question. Frontend runtime-state isolation (ruling 18,
2286-Python's `py.rt` cells) is a frontend and session concern outside the
2287-linker and is not designed here.
2288-
2289:#### 7.5.11 Exposure: the registry and its installation family
2290-
2291-Admission governs entry into a registry. An allow admits one address
2292-into the requesting task's registry, and authorization follows that
2293-registry and its installation-family inheritance:
2294-
2295-- `link-install` spawns the install child over the parent's linked
2296-  entries and receives each into it;
2297-- the child's installs transfer back to the parent with their
2298-  `:admission` record;
2299-- a name already resolvable in the registry answers with no link request
2300-  and hence no question.
2301-
2302-The exposure domain is therefore the installation family for its life,
2303-not one task. It is about principal plus address plus holder context,
2304-and it is language-blind: any frontend compiling to a require of that
2305-name hits the same entry.
2306-
2307-- **Untrust is not retroactive.** `(untrust p)` changes the decision
2308-  snapshot for questions not yet answered. It evicts no registry entry,
2309-  revokes no appended response, and leaves a question already answered
2310-  allow allowed.
2311-- `(reset)` and `(vm ...)` start a fresh registry and a fresh admission
2312-  state.
2313-
2314:#### 7.5.12 Trust versus safety
2315-
2316-Trust is which principals a holder accepts, and it is the admission
2317-interpreter's answer. Safety is the linker's and L-e's, enforced whatever
2318-the answer says: closure origin, store ownership, dependency pins (7.4)
2319-and the primitives profile (`yang.antlr.md` 8.5.6, ruling 19). **An allow
2320-never relaxes L-e.** The interpreter sees names, addresses, asserters and
2321-provenance, never bytes.
2322-
2323-**Conditions are reserved empty in v1.** `:yin.admit/conditions` must be
2324-`{}`. A primitive or effect bound on a trusted module is future work and
2325-does not land before L-e.
2326-
2327:#### 7.5.13 Fail closed
2328-
2329-Every reason below is final for that link:
2330-
2331-- A DHT link source with a nil `:admission` refuses
2332-  `:yin.admit/unconfigured`.
2333-- A malformed answer refuses `:yin.admit/malformed-answer`.
2334-- An answer with non-empty conditions refuses
2335-  `:yin.admit/unsupported-condition`.
2336-- An ended decision snapshot refuses `:yin.admit/decision-ended`.
2337-- The default interpreter refuses an untrusted asserter set
2338-  `:yin.admit/untrusted-principal`.
2339-
2340-An absent snapshot and an unanswered question never allow; they leave the
2341-link pending until an answer or retirement.
2342-
2343-**Must not land before L-e:** anything that claims an untrusted-origin
2344-import is safe; a default decision beyond own principals; honoring any
2345-non-empty condition; a weakening of profile, dependency-pin or
2346-export-origin checks; admission in a frontend or on the content pair.
2347-Everything here is AST-level data on streams, so the register-VM cutover
2348-does not affect it.
